ir feedback on the learning process.  Casal (2005) argued that CL has a positive impact on the learning of foreign languages due to theongoing exchange between students. Eskay et al. (2012) maintained that CL enhances academic per-formance by fostering the acquisition of social skills, such as conflict resolution, facilitating the integra-{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 6{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}comprehension skills at the undergraduate level. Chaves-Yuste \&amp; de-la-Pen {\textasciitilde}a (2022) and Dewi andHarlamu (2023) emphasized the importance of incorporating authentic materials into the CIRC techniqueto foster writing and reading skills. These findings collectively suggest that CIRC is a versatile and effect-ive approach across various educational levels and contexts. These classroom-based findings are directly relevant to the present study, which seeks to investigate{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 6{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}comprehension performance between the groups. This emphasizes the need to balance annotationquantity and quality in gamified learning environments. Maruf and Anjely (2020) studied the impact of the CIRC teaching strategy with mobile learning onIndonesian students’ reading comprehension, finding significant improvements. Fatmawati’s (2019)research on web-blog-assisted CIRC learning in physics education revealed that engaging in reading{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater