t-test, with{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 11{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}hension skills. The mean score increased from 12.12 in the pre-test to 33.89 in the post-test, withstandard deviations of 4.563 and 2.069, respectively. The t-value was 39.876, indicating a highly signifi-cant difference (p \&lt; 0.01), with a large effect size of 8.74 total reading comprehension skills. Another objective of the study was to find out if using the web-based CIRC technique made a signifi-cant difference in the literal comprehension sub-skills of first-year prep experimental language school{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 11{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}The means and t-values of the participants’ scores with Cohen’s d on the pre- and post-readingtests in literal comprehension sub-skillsIt is clear from Table 6 that for the literal sub-skill ‘determining the meaning of words from context’, the{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 11{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}mean score increased from 2.567 in the pre-test to 7.183 in the post-test, with a decrease in standarddeviations of 1.995 and 0.688. A statistically significant difference at the 0.01 level (t-value ¼ 13.205)indicates a highly significant difference (p \&lt; 0.01), with a large effect size (Cohen’s d ¼ 6.23) in fa