	abstract = {The aim of this study is the detection of malingering and dissimulation in the d2 test by means of certain test scores. A total of 94 university students worked on the test under three conditions. Their task consisted in achieving a good or a bad result by faking or in achieving a good result by effort alone. After subjects were familiar with the test they were given a short break to think about an effective strategy before working on the test again under the same condition. Even in the second trial results in the faking good condition did not differ significantly from the control condition. In the faking bad condition test scores exceeded those in the control condition about 20 standard Z-scores. Among the analyzed types of error the “letter errors” (p marked instead of d) proved to be the best indicator of malingering. When the critical score was set to 2 errors, 63 percent of the simulating subjects were detected (10 errors: 47 percent), while no misclassification of subjects in the control group occurred. A cross-validation of this indicator of faking was applied by analyzing tests available from 225 university students and 153 neurological patients. The criterion of 2 or more errors led to false classification of 3 percent of the subjects and 8 percent of the patients as fakers. When the limit was set to 10 errors, not a single false diagnosis was made. Thus, a sensitive and a less sensitive but highly specific criterion for the detection of malingering in the d2 test is available. The d2 test seems not to be very susceptible to dissimulation since subjects instructed to fake a good result did not outperform subjects in the control condition. The strategy of skipping items, as discussed in the literature, seems to be less important in the practical application of the test.},
