onal brick-and-mortar classrooms to onlinelearning can potentially impact students’ sense of identity as they navigate and adjustto the new virtual environment. This shift may lead them to compare their current{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 25{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}introduce bias and fail to capture the complex nature of identity changes in onlinelearning. Finally, the study’s emphasis on identity changes may have neglected othercomponents of the online learning experience that may have an impact on student’sacademic success and well-being. Thus, a request for more research is issued to dive deeper into the effects of online{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 26{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}Das, U. (2023). Online learning: Challenges and solutions for learners and teachers. Management and Labour Studies, 48(2), 210–213. https://doi.org/10.1177/0258042X211069501.Dawadi, S., Giri, R., \&amp; Simkhada, P. (2020). Impact of COVID-19 on the education sector in Nepal chal- lenges and coping strategies. Sage Submissions Preprint. https://doi.org/10.31124/advance.12344336. v1.{\textless}/pre{\textgreater}{\textless}/blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}/li{\textgreater}{\textless}li{\textgreater}{\textless}p{\textgreater}{\textless}strong{\textgreater}Seite 26{\textless}/strong{\textgreater}{\textless}/p{\textgreater}{\textless}blockquote{\textgreater}{\textless}pre{\textgreater}Dreamson, N. (2020). Online design education: Meta-connective pedagogy. International Journal of Art \&amp; Design Edu